.TH MURX 1 "November 2026" "murx" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
murx \- small persistent terminal multiplexer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B murx
.RB [ \-p " letter"]
.RB [ \-s " top|bottom"]
.RB [ \-c " RRGGBB"]
.RB [ \-\-cwd ]
.RB [ \-\-clock ]
.RB [ \-\-murx ]
.RB [ \-\-hostname ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ session ]
.br
.B murx
.BR \-h " | " \-\-help
.br
.B murx
.BR \-l " | " \-\-list\-sessions
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B murx
runs shells and terminal programs in windows and split panes.
It attaches to the named session, starting a server if necessary.
The default session name is
.BR 0 .
Each session has its own server and Unix socket.
Detaching leaves programs running; run the same command to reattach.
The session ends when its last pane exits or its server is killed.
murx refuses to start or attach inside another murx session; use
.B env \-u MURX murx
to override intentionally.
.PP
Multiple clients may attach simultaneously and share windows and the active
pane. Each client has its own prefix, menus, lock and scrollback view.
There is no configuration file or command language.
.PP
Options precede the session name. Names contain 1 to 48 letters, digits,
hyphens or underscores. Use
.B \-\-
before a name beginning with a hyphen.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list\-sessions
List running sessions for the current user, sorted by name, one per line.
Checks the runtime socket directory and /tmp fallback, skips stale sockets
and suppresses duplicate names. No terminal is required and no session is
created or attached. Prints nothing if no sessions are running.
Do not supply a session name with this option.
.TP
.BR \-p " letter, " \-\-prefix " letter"
Choose this client's control prefix. Letters a through z are accepted,
case-insensitively; a means Ctrl-A and x means Ctrl-X.
The default is Ctrl-A. The old C-x argument syntax is not accepted.
Repeat this option when reattaching, or set a shell alias.
Other clients may choose different prefixes.
.TP
.BR \-s " top|bottom, " \-\-status " top|bottom"
Place the status line at the top or bottom.
New sessions default to top. An explicit choice changes an existing session
for all clients. Omitting this option preserves the session's position.
Prompts and messages follow the status line.
.TP
.BR \-c " RRGGBB, " \-\-color " RRGGBB"
Set the session's status bar background for all attached clients.
Supply six hexadecimal digits, such as 000000 for black.
Omitting this option preserves the session's color when reattaching. New
sessions use the terminal's default background unless a color is supplied.
.TP
.B \-\-cwd
Show the current directory at the left of the status line.
.TP
.B \-\-clock
Show the status clock.
.TP
.B \-\-murx
Show the [murx] label at the left of the status line when
.B \-\-cwd
is not enabled. The directory takes precedence if both options are supplied.
.TP
.B \-\-hostname
Show the hostname: prefix in window names and the outer terminal title.
.TP
.B \-\-hide-pane-titles
Hide the title row above each pane. Pane titles are shown by default.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and keyboard shortcuts, then exit.
.PP
Display options set a new session's values; the Configuration menu can
change them in a running session. The cwd, clock, [murx] label and hostname
options are off by default; pane titles are shown by default.
To restore the previous default display, use
.BR "murx \-\-cwd \-\-clock \-\-hostname session" .
.SH KEY BINDINGS
Every binding below starts with the prefix: press Ctrl-A, release it,
then press the remaining key. Substitute your chosen prefix throughout,
including both keys of the last-window binding.
Prefix then a sends the chosen control prefix literally to the pane.
Doubling the prefix switches to the last window and takes precedence over
other control-key bindings. With Ctrl-X as prefix, Ctrl-X a sends Ctrl-X,
Ctrl-X Ctrl-X switches windows and Ctrl-X x locks the client.
Other Ctrl-A input is passed to the pane when a different prefix is selected.

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A m
open the main menu.
The menu has submenus for panes, sessions and configuration;
Ctrl\-A w opens the window list in the same style.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-A a
send the configured prefix literally to the pane.

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A c, Ctrl\-A Ctrl\-C
new window.

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A n, Ctrl\-A Space, Ctrl\-A Ctrl\-N, Ctrl\-A Ctrl\-@
next window.

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A p, Ctrl\-A Ctrl\-P, Ctrl\-A Backspace
previous window.

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A Ctrl\-A
last window.

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A w, Ctrl\-A Ctrl\-W, Ctrl\-A "
window menu (Up/Down, j/k, Enter).

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A A
temporarily rename window (until the next automatic name refresh).

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A 0 ... Ctrl\-A 9
select window by its number in the status line.

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A \-
split top/bottom.

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A |, Ctrl\-A %
split left/right.

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A h Ctrl\-A j Ctrl\-A k Ctrl\-A l, Ctrl\-A Up/Down/Left/Right
select pane.

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A Tab / Ctrl\-A Shift\-Tab
next / previous pane.

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A o
next pane.

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A Ctrl\-H Ctrl\-A Ctrl\-J Ctrl\-A Ctrl\-K Ctrl\-A Ctrl\-L
resize pane (8/5/5/8).

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A x, Ctrl\-A Ctrl\-X
lock the client.

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A Page Up, Ctrl\-A [
enter scrollback (Page Up also scrolls one page).

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A K
kill pane (confirm).

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A &
kill window (confirm).

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A \(rs
kill server (confirm).

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A r
show Configuration Reloaded!.

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A R
force the session to this client's terminal size.

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A *
list attached clients.

.TP
.B Ctrl\-A d, Ctrl\-A Ctrl\-D
detach.

.PP
Directional selection uses pane edges and prefers panes overlapping on the
perpendicular axis, then the nearest pane in the requested direction.
Resize steps are eight columns horizontally and five rows vertically.
Ctrl-A k selects the pane above; Ctrl-A K kills a pane.
Renaming a window is temporary until the next automatic name refresh.
Ctrl-A r only displays a message: there is no configuration file to reload.
.SS Menus, prompts and confirmations
The main menu offers new windows and panes, closing and renaming, the window
list, pane selection and resizing, and Session and Configuration submenus.
Use Up/Down or j/k to move and Enter to select. Left/Right or h/l enter and
leave submenus; q, Escape, or Ctrl-C closes the menu. Long menus scroll.
The Configuration submenu sets the status line position and background
(default, black or grey), the client prefix, display toggles for the directory,
clock, [murx] label and hostname, and resets display/status defaults (display toggles, status position and
background). Session
settings affect all attached clients; the prefix is per-client. Changes take
effect immediately and are not saved to disk.
In prompts use Enter to accept, Escape or Ctrl-C to cancel, Backspace or Ctrl-H
to delete, and Ctrl-U to clear.
Confirm destructive actions with y, Y or Enter; n, N, Escape or Ctrl-C cancels.
The client lock is cosmetic: Enter unlocks it without a password.
.SH SCROLLBACK
Ctrl-A Page Up freezes the active pane's history and moves up one page.
Ctrl-A [ opens the view at the current screen.
Use Page Up and Page Down without a prefix to browse;
q, Escape, Enter or Ctrl-C returns to live output.
The usual window shortcuts work in scrollback, including Ctrl-A n, Ctrl-A p,
Ctrl-A 0 through Ctrl-A 9, a double prefix, and Ctrl-A w for the window chooser.
Use Ctrl-A c to create a window while browsing.
Returning to a window restores its scrollback view and position.
.PP
Each pane retains up to 2,000 normal-screen history lines in memory.
Programs keep running while the view is frozen.
Scrollback views are private to each client and saved per pane while attached.
Alternate-screen programs such as editors do not add history;
opening scrollback there shows only their current screen.
Colors are retained; lines are clipped or padded to the pane width without reflow.
Text selection and copying are not provided.
.SH STATUS AND TERMINAL DISPLAY
With --cwd, the left side shows [/path/to/directory]. With --murx and without
--cwd, it shows [murx]. By default neither label is shown.
On Linux the directory follows the
active pane's foreground process, falling back to its shell and then the
session's starting directory. Other systems use the starting directory unless
a shell reports a path. Local HOME paths are abbreviated with ~.
Long paths end in dots to leave space for the window list and clock;
the label is hidden if fewer than four columns remain.
.PP
The window list is right-aligned. Each entry contains its number and name;
* marks the current window and a trailing hyphen marks the previous window.
Names show program for each window's active foreground job,
or hostname:program with --hostname,
including inactive windows, and refresh within one second.
Pipelines use the foreground process group leader's name.
Returning to the shell restores its name.
Linux uses /proc; other systems use ps.
With --clock, the right side shows HH:MM:SS, refreshed every second.
.PP
The outer terminal title follows [murx] program, or [murx] hostname:program
with --hostname. Its [murx] prefix is always present; --murx controls only
the status label.
Hostnames are shortened at the first dot.
By default each pane has a title row above its content showing
[R N: title R], where N is the pane number. Active pane titles use colour7
brackets/numbers, colour11 separator and colour15 title; inactive panes use
colour8, colour3 and colour12. Use --hide-pane-titles or the Configuration
menu to hide title rows. Splits use thin box-drawing lines in colour8.
The status uses colour12, accents colour14 and the current window colour11.
The current window name turns bright red (colour9) when its active pane's
foreground process is su or has effective UID 0, including programs launched
from a root shell. Inactive names retain colour12. The color refreshes within
one second and returns to yellow for an unprivileged active pane.
Over SSH the shell must report root status; install the hooks in root's shell
as well as the regular user's. This is a visual hint, not a security indicator.
Borders use colour8 on the terminal's default background.
Messages use foreground colour6 and background colour8.
Menus use a black background with a colour12 frame, colour11 titles and
selection, colour14 accents and dim colour8 separators and help text.
.PP
UTF-8, wide characters, application cursor keys, 256 colors and alternate
screens are supported. Output uses 256-color SGR by default. RGB pane colours
are passed through as 24-bit SGR when COLORTERM is truecolor or 24bit and TERM
does not start with screen, tmux, linux, or dumb; otherwise RGB is mapped to
the nearest xterm-256 entry.
.SH SESSION SIZE
The most recently attached or resized client determines the session size,
clamped to a minimum of 20 columns by 5 rows and a maximum of 1,000 by 1,000.
Larger clients center the session; smaller clients show its top-left portion.
Ctrl-A R forces the session to the current client's terminal size.
Ctrl-A * lists attached clients for size diagnostics.
.PP
Terminal size changes are checked every 250 milliseconds as well as on SIGWINCH.
Resizing does not reflow existing text.
.SH REMOTE SHELL INTEGRATION
Enable --hostname to display reported remote hostnames and --cwd to display
reported directories when creating the session. The hostname examples below
assume --hostname is enabled.
.PP
SSH does not expose remote foreground programs or working directories locally.
Configure the remote shell to send terminal reports using one of the examples
below. Reload the shell configuration or open a new SSH shell afterward.
Restart session servers after upgrading murx to gain newly added features.
.PP
OSC 0 or OSC 2 titles of the form hostname:program update the SSH window name.
Use a hostname containing only letters, digits, dots, underscores or hyphens,
and a program basename without whitespace, slashes or colons.
Without a matching report, the name stays local-hostname:ssh.
Structured remote titles are retained for the SSH job even if a program
subsequently sends an unstructured title.
.PP
Directories may be reported using OSC 7:
.nf
ESC ] 7 ; file://host/percent-encoded-path BEL
.fi
or using the murx-specific form:
.nf
ESC ] 777 ; murx-cwd ; /absolute/path BEL
ESC ] 777 ; murx-cwd ; ~/relative/to/remote/home BEL
.fi
The spaces above separate fields for readability and are not transmitted.
ESC backslash also terminates an OSC report.
Standard OSC 7 paths are percent-decoded and displayed as absolute paths;
the murx-specific form preserves remote ~ abbreviations.
Reports are associated with the foreground job that sent them.
Disconnecting SSH restores the local shell's path and name.
With splits the window and status follow the active pane.
OSC 777;murx-root;1 reports a root shell and OSC 777;murx-root;0 clears it.
The report is tied to the foreground SSH job and is ignored after disconnect.
Remote su command titles also mark the active window red, even before a UID
report arrives. Remote shells without hooks cannot report their UID.
Install the matching hook in both the regular user's and root's startup files.
The examples activate in interactive screen-256color terminals as well as when
SSH_CONNECTION is set, so su and sudo login shells can report status even if
they clear the SSH environment. The parent shell clears root status at its
next prompt after the root shell exits.
.SS Zsh
Add the following to ~/.zshrc on the remote host you connect to over SSH.
The preexec hook reports the first command of a pipeline;
precmd restores the shell name and reports the directory after cd.
.nf
.ft CR
if [[ \-o interactive && ( \-n $SSH_CONNECTION || $TERM == screen\-256color ) ]]; then
    autoload \-Uz add\-zsh\-hook
    murx_ssh_title() {
        local program=${1:t}
        printf '\e033]2;%s:%s\e007' "${HOST%%.*}" "$program"
        local directory=$PWD
        if [[ $directory == "$HOME" ]]; then
            directory='~'
        elif [[ $directory == "$HOME/"* ]]; then
            directory="~/${directory#"$HOME/"}"
        fi
        printf '\e033]777;murx\-cwd;%s\e007' "$directory"
        printf '\e033]777;murx\-root;%s\e007' "$((EUID == 0))"
    }
    murx_ssh_preexec() {
        local \-a words
        words=(${(z)1})
        murx_ssh_title "${(Q)words[1]}"
    }
    murx_ssh_precmd() { murx_ssh_title zsh; }
    add\-zsh\-hook preexec murx_ssh_preexec
    add\-zsh\-hook precmd murx_ssh_precmd
fi

.ft
.fi
.SS Bash
Add the following to ~/.bashrc on the remote host you connect to over SSH.
It preserves string and array PROMPT_COMMAND values and avoids duplicate
installation when sourced again. It installs a DEBUG trap: if an existing
framework such as bash-preexec already owns that trap, integrate the functions
with that framework instead of replacing its trap.
Do not enable function tracing (set -T) for these hooks.
.nf
.ft CR
if [[ $\- == *i* && ( \-n $SSH_CONNECTION || $TERM == screen\-256color ) &&
      \-z ${murx_ssh_installed\-} ]]; then
    murx_ssh_title() {
        local program=$1 directory=$PWD
        program=${program##*/}
        printf '\e033]2;%s:%s\e007' "${HOSTNAME%%.*}" "$program"
        if [[ $directory == "$HOME" ]]; then
            directory='~'
        elif [[ $directory == "$HOME/"* ]]; then
            directory="~/${directory#"$HOME/"}"
        fi
        printf '\e033]777;murx\-cwd;%s\e007' "$directory"
        printf '\e033]777;murx\-root;%s\e007' "$((EUID == 0))"
    }
    murx_ssh_preexec() {
        if [[ ${murx_ssh_ready\-0} == 1 && $1 != murx_ssh_precmd ]]; then
            murx_ssh_ready=0
            local program rest
            read \-r program rest <<< "$1"
            # Strip quotes around a simple command name without evaluating it.
            program=${program#\e"}; program=${program%\e"}
            program=${program#\e'}; program=${program%\e'}
            murx_ssh_title "$program"
        fi
        return 0
    }
    murx_ssh_precmd() {
        local last_status=$?
        murx_ssh_title bash
        murx_ssh_ready=1
        return "$last_status"
    }
    # Keep existing prompt commands, and run our prompt hook last.
    if [[ $(declare \-p PROMPT_COMMAND 2>/dev/null) == 'declare \-a '* ]]; then
        PROMPT_COMMAND+=(murx_ssh_precmd)
    else
        PROMPT_COMMAND=("${PROMPT_COMMAND\-}" murx_ssh_precmd)
    fi
    trap 'murx_ssh_preexec "$BASH_COMMAND"' DEBUG
    murx_ssh_installed=1
fi

.ft
.fi

.PP
The Bash command name is the first whitespace-delimited word of the first
simple command. Aliases, assignments, compound commands and quoted executable
paths containing spaces may display differently from the Zsh hook.
Command text is never evaluated by these hooks.
Both examples emit invisible terminal updates rather than visible command lines.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MURX
Set inside panes to the session name. murx refuses to start or attach when
this variable is set, preventing nested sessions. Use
.B env \-u MURX murx
to override intentionally.
.TP
.B SHELL
Login shell started in new panes. If unset or empty, /usr/bin/zsh is used
when executable, otherwise /bin/sh.
Panes receive TERM=screen-256color and inherit the server's environment and umask.
.TP
.B HOME
Home directory abbreviated as ~ in locally discovered status paths.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Preferred parent directory for session sockets.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Socket directory parent if XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is unset or empty;
/tmp is used if neither is set.
.TP
.BR TERM ", " COLORTERM
Describe the attaching terminal and affect truecolor output.
.TP
.B SSH_CONNECTION
Used by the example shell hooks to enable reporting in remote SSH shells.
.TP
.B LC_CTYPE
Along with the standard locale environment, controls character width handling.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/murx-<uid>/<session>.sock
Per-user session socket. If XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is unavailable, the parent comes
from TMPDIR or /tmp. Long socket paths fall back to /tmp/murx-<uid>/.
Socket permissions are restricted to the owner.
.PP
There is no configuration file and no on-disk scrollback.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start or reattach to the default session:
.PP
.nf
murx
.fi
.PP
Use a named session, Ctrl-X prefix and bottom status:
.PP
.nf
murx \-\-prefix x \-\-status bottom work
.fi
.PP
Start a session with a minimal status display:
.PP
.nf
murx minimal
.fi
.PP
Detach with Ctrl-A d (or Ctrl-X d with the alternate prefix), then run the
same session command to reattach. Exit the last pane's shell to end the session,
or use the confirmed kill-pane, kill-window or kill-server bindings.
.SH UPGRADING FROM MOERX
The executable and manual are now murx and murx(1). Update scripts and aliases,
and remove the old status-label disabling option. The label is now disabled
by default; use \fB\-\-murx\fR to enable it.
Sessions use a separate murx-<uid> socket directory and are not migrated.
Finish existing sessions with the old executable before removing it.
Replace optional remote shell hooks and reload the remote shell;
custom OSC reports now use murx-cwd and murx-root.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal client exit, including detaching, successful help output or session
listing (including an empty list).
.TP
.B 1
Unable to start or attach to a session, client terminal setup failed, or
a session directory could not be read while listing.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line options or session name.
.PP
This is the client's status, not the exit status of programs running in panes.
.SH LIMITATIONS
No configuration file, command prompt, scripting interface, plugins, pane zoom,
activity monitoring, clock mode or scrollback copying is provided.
Mouse reporting is not negotiated with the outer terminal.
The lock screen is not a security mechanism.
New windows and panes start in the server's original working directory,
not the active pane's current directory.
There is one resize step per key press, without a dedicated repeat mode.
At most 60 panes may be created in a window.
Killing a server affects only its own named session.
